var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"api/multispecies_equations/#Multi-species-equations","page":"Multi-species equations","text":"CompressibleEulerEquationsMs1T2D are the compressible Euler equations for multi-species flows of calorically imperfect gases in thermal equilibrium, based on the tabulated data of ThermoData1T, with entropy-conservative fluxes and slip-wall boundary conditions.","title":"Multi-species equations"},{"category":"type","location":"api/multispecies_equations/#AeroTrixi.CompressibleEulerEquationsMs1T2D","page":"Multi-species equations","text":"CompressibleEulerEquationsMs1T2D(ref_q, mass_arr, e_int_function, c_int_function;\n                                 T_min = 10.0, T_max = 3.0e4, T_tol = 1e-9,\n                                 dT = 1.0, min_T_jump = 1e-6,\n                                 interpolation = :linear, cv_table_offset = false)\n\nMulticomponent compressible Euler equations in two space dimensions for a flow in thermal equilibrium, i.e. a single temperature T shared by all degrees of freedom of all species:\n\nfracpartialpartial t\nbeginpmatrix\nrho v_1  rho v_2  rho e_texttotal  rho_1  vdots  rho_n\nendpmatrix\n+\nfracpartialpartial x\nbeginpmatrix\nrho v_1^2 + p  rho v_1 v_2  ( rho e_texttotal + p) v_1  rho_1 v_1  vdots  rho_n v_1\nendpmatrix\n+\nfracpartialpartial y\nbeginpmatrix\nrho v_1 v_2  rho v_2^2 + p  ( rho e_texttotal + p) v_2  rho_1 v_2  vdots  rho_n v_2\nendpmatrix\n=\nbeginpmatrix\n0  0  0  0  vdots  0\nendpmatrix\n\nHere rho_i is the density of species i, rho = sum_i=1^n rho_i, v_1, v_2 the velocities and e_texttotal the specific total energy. Unlike a calorically perfect gas there is no constant gamma: the pressure follows from the number density rather than from the internal energy,\n\np = n k_B T qquad n = sum_i=1^n fracrho_im_i\n\nand the internal energy is tabulated,\n\nrho e_texttotal = sum_i=1^n rho_i e_i(T) + frac12 rho (v_1^2 + v_2^2)\n\nwith e_i(T) carrying the translational, rotational and vibrational contributions of species i. Recovering T from e therefore requires a Newton iteration against the tables, which are held in a ThermoData1T instance in the thermodata field.\n\nThe conservative variables are (rho v_1 rho v_2 rho e_texttotal rho_1 ldots rho_n) — note that the momenta come first, there is no rho entry — and the primitive variables are (v_1 v_2 T rho_1 ldots rho_n), with T in place of the pressure.\n\nAll quantities are non-dimensionalised by ref_q, so k_B = 1 in the equations as implemented and p = n T.\n\nflux_oblapenko_etal is an entropy-conservative two-point flux for this system, available for both an orientation and a normal_direction.\n\nArguments\n\nref_q: ReferenceFlowQuantities (see FlowRef.jl) used to scale every quantity.\nmass_arr: species masses in kg. Not modified.\ne_int_function, c_int_function: one callable of T per species returning the   specific internal energy and specific heat of the internal degrees of freedom;   the translational parts are added internally by ThermoData1T.\nT_min, T_max, dT: tabulation range and step, in K. Temperatures are clamped   to 10001T_min 09999T_max.\nT_tol: relative tolerance of the Newton solver for T(e).\nmin_T_jump: in units of dT. Below this temperature jump flux_oblapenko_etal   replaces its divided differences by the equivalent midpoint expressions, which   avoids the 00 as T_rr to T_ll.\ninterpolation: only :linear is implemented.\ncv_table_offset: tabulate c_v on a grid shifted by -Delta T2, see   ThermoData1T.\n\nReferences\n\nOblapenko, Torrilhon (2025). Computers and Fluids, 106640.   DOI: 10.1016/j.compfluid.2025.106640\nOblapenko, Tarnovskiy, Ertl, Torrilhon (2024). STAB/DGLR Symposium.   DOI: 10.1007/978-3-032-11115-9_36\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AeroTrixi.CompressibleEulerEquationsMs1T2D"},{"category":"method","location":"api/multispecies_equations/#AeroTrixi.flux_oblapenko_etal-Tuple{Any, Any, Integer, CompressibleEulerEquationsMs1T2D}","page":"Multi-species equations","text":"flux_oblapenko_etal(u_ll, u_rr, orientation_or_normal_direction,\n                    equations::CompressibleEulerEquationsMs1T2D)\n\nThis flux is the multi-species entropy-conservative flux described in\n\nGeorgii Oblapenko, Manuel Torrilhon (2025) Entropy-conservative high-order methods for high-enthalpy gas flows DOI: 10.1016/j.compfluid.2025.106640\n\nThe multi-species version is also described in\n\nGeorgii Oblapenko, Arseniy Tarnovskiy, Moritz Ertl, Manuel Torrilhon (2026) Entropy-Stable Fluxes for High-Order Discontinuous Galerkin Simulations of High-Enthalpy Flows DOI: 10.1007/978-3-032-11115-9_36\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AeroTrixi.flux_oblapenko_etal"},{"category":"method","location":"api/multispecies_equations/#AeroTrixi.flux_oblapenko_etal_taylor-Tuple{Any, Any, Integer, CompressibleEulerEquationsMs1T2D}","page":"Multi-species equations","text":"flux_oblapenko_etal_taylor(u_ll, u_rr, orientation_or_normal_direction,\n                    equations::CompressibleEulerEquationsMs1T2D)\n\nThis flux is a faster approximate version of the multi-species entropy-conservative flux described in\n\nGeorgii Oblapenko, Manuel Torrilhon (2025) Entropy-conservative high-order methods for high-enthalpy gas flows DOI: 10.1016/j.compfluid.2025.106640\n\nThe approximation is described in\n\nBeatriz Fagá, Georgii Oblapenko, Yiyang Zhu, Daniel Doehring, Manuel Torrilhon (2026) Efficient high-order Discontinuous Galerkin simulations of multi-species high-enthalpy flows DOI: 10.48550/arXiv.2611.12511\n\nThe multi-species version is also described in\n\nGeorgii Oblapenko, Arseniy Tarnovskiy, Moritz Ertl, Manuel Torrilhon (2026) Entropy-Stable Fluxes for High-Order Discontinuous Galerkin Simulations of High-Enthalpy Flows DOI: 10.1007/978-3-032-11115-9_36\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AeroTrixi.flux_oblapenko_etal_taylor"},{"category":"method","location":"api/multispecies_equations/#Trixi.boundary_condition_slip_wall-Tuple{Any, AbstractVector, Any, Any, Any, Any, CompressibleEulerEquationsMs1T2D}","page":"Multi-species equations","text":"boundary_condition_slip_wall(u_inner, normal_direction, direction, x, t,\n                                surface_flux_function, equations::CompressibleEulerEquationsMs1T2D)\n\nShould be used together with StructuredMesh.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Trixi.boundary_condition_slip_wall"},{"category":"method","location":"api/multispecies_equations/#Trixi.boundary_condition_slip_wall-Tuple{Any, AbstractVector, Any, Any, Any, CompressibleEulerEquationsMs1T2D}","page":"Multi-species equations","text":"boundary_condition_slip_wall(u_inner, normal_direction::AbstractVector,\n                             x, t,\n                             surface_flux_function,\n                             equations::CompressibleEulerEquationsMs1T2D)\n\nDetermine the boundary numerical surface flux for a slip wall condition. Imposes a zero normal velocity at the wall. Density is taken from the internal solution state and pressure is computed as an exact solution of a 1D Riemann problem. Further details about this boundary state are available in the paper:\n\nJ. J. W. van der Vegt and H. van der Ven (2002) Slip flow boundary conditions in discontinuous Galerkin discretizations of the Euler equations of gas dynamics PDF\n\nDetails about the 1D pressure Riemann solution can be found in Section 6.3.3 of the book\n\nEleuterio F. Toro (2009) Riemann Solvers and Numerical Methods for Fluid Dynamics: A Practical Introduction 3rd edition DOI: 10.1007/b79761\n\nThe implementation is modified to ensure non-negativity of p_star. This modification preserves entropy stability based on the analysis in the paper:\n\nF. J. Hindenlang, G. J. Gassner, D. A. Kopriva (2020) Stability of wall boundary condition procedures for discontinuous Galerkin spectral element approximations of the compressible Euler equations DOI: 10.1007/978-3-030-39647-3_1\n\nShould be used together with UnstructuredMesh2D, P4estMesh, or T8codeMesh.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Trixi.boundary_condition_slip_wall"},{"category":"method","location":"api/multispecies_equations/#Trixi.boundary_condition_slip_wall-Tuple{Any, Any, Any, Any, Any, Any, CompressibleEulerEquationsMs1T2D}","page":"Multi-species equations","text":"boundary_condition_slip_wall(u_inner, orientation, direction, x, t,\n                                surface_flux_function, equations::CompressibleEulerEquationsMs1T2D)\n\nShould be used together with TreeMesh.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Trixi.boundary_condition_slip_wall"},{"category":"section","location":"trixi_gridap_bridge/#Trixi.jl–Gridap.jl-bridge","page":"Trixi.jl-Gridap.jl bridge","text":"The bridge of AeroTrixi.jl couples the discontinuous Galerkin (DG) solver of Trixi.jl with continuous finite elements of Gridap.jl on the same, adaptively refined P4estMesh. A typical use is a hyperbolic system that is coupled to an elliptic equation, for example the Euler equations with an electrostatic potential: in each time step (or in each Runge-Kutta stage), a source is taken from the DG solution, a finite element problem is solved with Gridap.jl, and the result is written back into the DG solution, where it enters the source terms of the flow.\n\nGridap.jl assembles the finite element problems from their weak form, so that other problems than the Poisson problem of the example (systems, other operators, other boundary conditions) can be set up with little effort. The bridge itself only provides the mesh, the node numbering with hanging-node constraints, the data transfer, and a callback for the time integration.","title":"Trixi.jl–Gridap.jl bridge"},{"category":"section","location":"trixi_gridap_bridge/#Current-Scope-and-Limitations","page":"Trixi.jl-Gridap.jl bridge","text":"The bridge currently covers:\n\n2D, P4estMesh with adaptive refinement, DG with polynomial degree 2 and continuous Q2 elements on the same nodes; serial runs only.\nMeshes with the full connectivity between trees, including corners.\nAn elliptic part of the equations with a single scalar Q2 field.\nLinear elliptic problems with homogeneous Dirichlet conditions, whose linear form is affine in the sources taken from the DG solution.\nFor the full order in time, a time integration method with stage_limiter! (SSPRK and low-storage Runge-Kutta methods of OrdinaryDiffEq.jl); with other methods, the elliptic problem can only be solved once per time step (first order in time).","title":"Current Scope and Limitations"},{"category":"section","location":"trixi_gridap_bridge/#Overview:-the-flow-of-a-run","page":"Trixi.jl-Gridap.jl bridge","text":"The diagram follows a simulation from the setup through the rebuilds after mesh changes to the Runge-Kutta stages of a time step. Each lane is one code base, time runs downward, and an arrow means \"calls or uses\":\n\nOrdinaryDiffEq.jl (grey): the time integration, which calls the stage update of the bridge as stage_limiter! on each stage;\nApplication (violet): what an example provides, i.e., the mesh (from an .inp file) and the equations with their fluxes, source terms, and elliptic part (fields, sources, weak forms, and how the solution is written into the DG state); the Euler-Poisson example serves as illustration;\nTrixi.jl (blue): the DG solver, the right-hand side, and the mesh adaptation;\nBridge tools (pink): the generic parts of the bridge in AeroTrixi.jl;\np4est (amber): the forest, its connectivity and adaptation, and the node numbering p4est_lnodes;\nGridap.jl (green): the model, the finite element spaces, assembly, and solution.\n\n(Image: Flow of a Trixi.jl run with a Gridap.jl problem)","title":"Overview: the flow of a run"},{"category":"section","location":"trixi_gridap_bridge/#Foundations","page":"Trixi.jl-Gridap.jl bridge","text":"","title":"Foundations"},{"category":"section","location":"trixi_gridap_bridge/#Matching-nodes:-DG-with-polydeg-2-and-Q2-elements","page":"Trixi.jl-Gridap.jl bridge","text":"The DG solution of Trixi.jl with polynomial degree 2 lives at the 3 × 3 Legendre-Gauss-Lobatto nodes of each element, which in reference coordinates are \\{-1, 0, 1\\}^2. These are exactly the nodes of the continuous Q2 Lagrange element on the same quadrilateral. Each DG element is therefore one Q2 element, and all transfers between the two discretizations work node by node, without interpolation or projection:\n\nDG → Q2: the value at a Q2 node is the average of the DG values of all elements that share the node. The values at hanging nodes are then set from the hanging-node constraints, so that the result is a continuous Q2 function.\nQ2 → DG: the Q2 value is injected at each DG node. Gradients are computed element by element with the derivative matrix and the metric terms of Trixi.jl; they are discontinuous across elements, as expected for the gradient of a continuous Q2 function.","title":"Matching nodes: DG with polydeg = 2 and Q2 elements"},{"category":"section","location":"trixi_gridap_bridge/#Continuous-node-numbering-from-p4est_lnodes","page":"Trixi.jl-Gridap.jl bridge","text":"p4est creates a continuous node numbering of degree 2 for the forest of the P4estMesh (p4est_lnodes_new with degree 2). P4estLnodesQ2 turns it into the data needed on both sides:\n\nelement_nodes: the 9 nodes of each element, in the lexicographic order of the DG nodes of Trixi.jl (i fastest), so that DG node (i, j) of element e is element_nodes[i + 3 * (j - 1), e];\ncoordinates of all nodes;\nthe hanging nodes with their constraints (see below); they are numbered after the n_independent independent nodes;\nboundary labels: the boundary names of the P4estMesh (as used by Trixi.jl for boundary conditions) are attached to the boundary nodes. Nodes on more than one named boundary (corners of the box) get their own entity with all names.\n\np4est_lnodes needs the full connectivity between the trees, including the corners. Meshes from .inp files are complete (p4est_connectivity_read_inp completes the connectivity). P4estMesh(trees_per_dimension; ...) of Trixi.jl does not contain the corner connectivity; use p4est_mesh_from_vertices, which completes the connectivity with p4est_connectivity_complete, or the test mesh p4est_mesh_five_trees of the box [-1, 1]^2 (a center square and four trapezoids, with bilinear trees).","title":"Continuous node numbering from p4est_lnodes"},{"category":"section","location":"trixi_gridap_bridge/#Hanging-nodes","page":"Trixi.jl-Gridap.jl bridge","text":"The P4estMesh of Trixi.jl is 2:1 balanced, so a coarse face meets at most two fine faces. On a coarse face with the Q2 nodes a, m (midpoint), b, the fine faces carry the nodes a, q_1, m, q_2, b. The node at m is the coarse midpoint itself; the quarter points are hanging nodes with the constraint of the quadratic interpolation on the coarse face,\n\nu(q_1) = tfrac38 u(a) + tfrac34 u(m) - tfrac18 u(b)\n\nWith 2:1 balance, the masters of a hanging node are never hanging themselves, so the constraints are explicit. apply_hanging_constraints! sets the values of all hanging nodes from their masters.","title":"Hanging nodes"},{"category":"section","location":"trixi_gridap_bridge/#Gridap.jl-model-and-space","page":"Trixi.jl-Gridap.jl bridge","text":"gridap_model creates a Gridap.jl UnstructuredDiscreteModel from the element corners, with face labels \"interior\", \"boundary\", and the boundary names of the mesh. (Gridap.jl would otherwise treat the faces at hanging nodes as boundary faces.)\n\ngridap_q2_space creates the Q2 space with the node numbering of P4estLnodesQ2: an unconstrained space with all nodes as degrees of freedom, and the space FESpaceWithLinearConstraints that eliminates the hanging nodes with their constraints. Nodes with a boundary name in dirichlet_tags are Dirichlet nodes. Symmetric problems stay symmetric after the elimination; the matrix of the Poisson problem with Dirichlet conditions is symmetric positive definite and can be factorized with Cholesky.","title":"Gridap.jl model and space"},{"category":"section","location":"trixi_gridap_bridge/#Interface","page":"Trixi.jl-Gridap.jl bridge","text":"Function Purpose\np4est_mesh_from_vertices, p4est_mesh_five_trees P4estMesh with full face and corner connectivity\nP4estLnodesQ2 continuous Q2 node numbering, hanging nodes, boundary labels\napply_hanging_constraints! values at hanging nodes from their masters\ngridap_model, gridap_q2_space Gridap.jl model and Q2 space (with constraints and Dirichlet nodes)\ngridap_q2_function, gridap_node_values node values → Gridap.jl function and back\ntransfer_dg_to_q2! DG solution → node values (averaging, constraints)\ntransfer_q2_to_dg!, q2_value, q2_gradient node values → DG solution (injection, element-wise gradients)\nGridapCallback, gridap_stage_update finite element solves during the time integration\nelliptic_fields, elliptic_sources, elliptic_forms, write_elliptic_solution, elliptic_spd, Q2Field elliptic part of equations\nCompressibleEulerPoissonEquations2D, source_terms_euler_poisson Euler equations with electrostatic potential\n\nThe Gridap.jl parts are a package extension of AeroTrixi.jl, which is loaded together with Gridap.jl. Load Gridap.jl with import Gridap and import only the names you need, e.g., using Gridap: ∫, ∇, ⋅; with using Gridap, names such as solve clash with Trixi.jl and OrdinaryDiffEq.jl.","title":"Interface"},{"category":"section","location":"trixi_gridap_bridge/#Elliptic-part-of-the-equations","page":"Trixi.jl-Gridap.jl bridge","text":"Like fluxes and source terms, the elliptic problem belongs to the equations. Equations with an elliptic part implement, dispatched on their type:\n\nelliptic_fields(equations): the unknowns and their spaces, e.g., (phi = Q2Field(),);\nelliptic_sources(u, x, equations): the source fields at a DG node;\nelliptic_forms(equations, gridap): the weak forms (a, l), written for tuples of fields;\nwrite_elliptic_solution(u, q, x, equations): the new state at a DG node from the solution;\noptionally elliptic_spd(equations): true for a symmetric positive definite matrix (Cholesky instead of LU).\n\nThe weak forms use Gridap.jl notation, but the equations do not depend on Gridap.jl: the bridge passes the Gridap.jl module as the argument gridap, and the forms take the operators from it. For the Euler-Poisson equations, all parts are in the file of the equations, src/equations/compressible_euler_poisson_2d.jl:\n\nelliptic_fields(::CompressibleEulerPoissonEquations2D) = (phi = Q2Field(),)\nelliptic_spd(::CompressibleEulerPoissonEquations2D) = true\n\nelliptic_sources(u, x, equations::CompressibleEulerPoissonEquations2D) =\n    SVector(u[1] - equations.rho_0)\n\nfunction elliptic_forms(::CompressibleEulerPoissonEquations2D, gridap)\n    ∫, ∇, ⋅ = gridap.∫, gridap.∇, gridap.:⋅\n    a((φ,), (v,), dΩ) = ∫(∇(v) ⋅ ∇(φ)) * dΩ\n    l((v,), sources, dΩ) = ∫(v * sources[1]) * dΩ\n    return a, l\nend\n\nfunction write_elliptic_solution(u, q, x, equations::CompressibleEulerPoissonEquations2D)\n    ∇φ = q2_gradient(q)\n    return with_electrostatic(euler_variables(u), SVector(q2_value(q), -∇φ[1], -∇φ[2]))\nend\n\nWith this, GridapCallback(semi; dirichlet_tags) solves the elliptic part of the equations of semi (see below).\n\nSince the forms are written for tuples of fields, a system has the same shape. For the Stokes problem with a velocity and a pressure, for example, the fields would be (velocity = Q2Field(components = 2), pressure = ...) and the bilinear form a((u, p), (v, q), dΩ) = ∫(∇(v) ⊙ ∇(u) - (∇ ⋅ v) * p + q * (∇ ⋅ u)) * dΩ. The bridge currently supports a single scalar Q2 field; vector-valued Q2 fields, a Q1 pressure space, and multi-field spaces are the next steps for such systems.","title":"Elliptic part of the equations"},{"category":"section","location":"trixi_gridap_bridge/#User-functions-of-the-transfers","page":"Trixi.jl-Gridap.jl bridge","text":"The transfers take pointwise functions, similar to initial conditions and source terms in Trixi.jl (elliptic_sources and write_elliptic_solution are such functions):\n\nsource(u, x, equations) returns an SVector of fields at a DG node, e.g., SVector(u[1] - ρ0); transfer_dg_to_q2! writes them into a matrix n_fields × n_nodes.\nwrite(u, q, x, equations) returns the new state at a DG node; q2_value(q, k) and q2_gradient(q, k) give the Q2 field k and its gradient at this node. transfer_q2_to_dg! writes the state directly into the DG solution vector.","title":"User functions of the transfers"},{"category":"section","location":"trixi_gridap_bridge/#Assembly-and-solution","page":"Trixi.jl-Gridap.jl bridge","text":"After each mesh change, the GridapCallback assembles the matrix of the bilinear form of elliptic_forms, with homogeneous Dirichlet conditions on the boundaries in dirichlet_tags, and factorizes it: with Cholesky if elliptic_spd is true, with LU otherwise. The linear form must be affine in the sources; then the load vector is b = b_0 + \\sum_k B_k f_k for the source values f_k at the nodes, and the callback also assembles b_0 and the matrices B_k once per mesh (checked against a direct assembly with random sources). Each solve then only needs sparse matrix-vector products and the solve with the factorization.","title":"Assembly and solution"},{"category":"section","location":"trixi_gridap_bridge/#Time-integration:-callback-and-stage-updates","page":"Trixi.jl-Gridap.jl bridge","text":"callback = GridapCallback(semi; dirichlet_tags = (:x_neg, :x_pos, :y_neg, :y_pos))\n\nsolves the elliptic part of the equations of semi. On its own, the callback solves the problem at the start and after every interval time steps. The solution is then constant during the stages of a Runge-Kutta step, which limits the coupled time integration to first order in time.\n\nFor the full order, the problem is solved on each Runge-Kutta stage. The SSPRK and low-storage Runge-Kutta methods of OrdinaryDiffEq.jl call a stage_limiter! on each stage value before the right-hand side is evaluated, and on the solution at the end of each step; gridap_stage_update provides it:\n\nstage_update = gridap_stage_update(callback)\nsol = solve(ode, CarpenterKennedy2N54(; stage_limiter! = stage_update,\n                                      williamson_condition = false); ...)\n\n(With williamson_condition = true, the stage limiter is not called. In OrdinaryDiffEqCore.jl v4, the stage limiter can also be passed to solve as the keyword stage_limiter.) The callback then only acts at the start and after mesh changes, where it rebuilds the mesh data and updates the solution on the new mesh. Mesh changes are detected with the revision counter of p4est; in the CallbackSet, the GridapCallback must come after the AMRCallback.\n\nThe difference in accuracy, for the Euler-Poisson equations with coupling on a fixed mesh (error of the Euler variables against a reference with a much smaller time step):\n\ntime steps update per step update per stage\n10 3.4e-4 1.6e-6\n20 1.7e-4 1.0e-7\n40 8.9e-5 6.9e-9\n80 4.5e-5 5.8e-10\norder 1 4","title":"Time integration: callback and stage updates"},{"category":"section","location":"trixi_gridap_bridge/#Example:-Euler-Poisson-blast-wave","page":"Trixi.jl-Gridap.jl bridge","text":"The Euler-Poisson equations CompressibleEulerPoissonEquations2D describe a charged gas, i.e., a single charged species in a uniform neutralizing background of density \\rho_0, under its own electrostatic field:\n\nbeginaligned\npartial_t rho + nabla cdot (rho v) = 0 \npartial_t (rho v) + nabla cdot (rho v otimes v + p I) = c  rho E \npartial_t (rho e) + nabla cdot ((rho e + p) v) = c  rho v cdot E \n-Delta phi = rho - rho_0 qquad E = -nabla phi\nendaligned\n\nThe field accelerates the gas c rho E and does work on it c rho v cdot E for c  0, it pushes the gas from regions of excess density towards regions of deficit. With the charge-to-mass ratio qm of the species and the permittivity varepsilon_0, the physical potential is phi_mathrmphys = (qm)  varepsilon_0  phi, and the coupling constant is\n\nc = frac(qm)^2varepsilon_0 = fracomega_p^2rho_0\n\nwith the plasma frequency omega_p of the background. Small density perturbations oscillate with omega_p. For c = 300 and rho_0 = 1, the period is 2pi  omega_p approx 036. A negative coupling c = -4 pi G describes self-gravity instead (attraction instead of repulsion).\n\nThe example elixir_euler_poisson_blast_five_trees_amr.jl combines all parts:\n\nCompressibleEulerPoissonEquations2D with the variables (rho rho v_1 rho v_2 rho e phi E_1 E_2); the potential and the field have no flux in the DG discretization, they are only set by the bridge. The force of the field on the flow, source_terms_euler_poisson, is scaled by coupling = 300 in the example. The field repels density excess, and its force is then comparable to the pressure forces of the blast: compared with the uncoupled case, the shock is weaker, the rarefied core refills, and the potential and the field oscillate in rings.\nA pressure blast (pressure 3 inside the radius 0.3, 1 outside, constant density) on the five-tree mesh of [-1, 1]^2, DG with polydeg = 2, shock capturing, and adaptive mesh refinement between the levels 1 and 5.\nThe Poisson problem -\\Delta\\phi = \\rho - \\rho_0 (the elliptic part of the equations, with the background density rho_0) with \\phi = 0 on the boundary, solved on each Runge-Kutta stage, with the field E = -\\nabla\\phi.\n\nThe example needs, besides AeroTrixi.jl, the packages Trixi.jl, OrdinaryDiffEqLowStorageRK.jl, Gridap.jl, and Plots.jl (for the plots below). Install them once in a separate environment for runs, e.g., in the folder run/ of the clone of AeroTrixi.jl, which git ignores. Do not add them to the environment of the package itself (julia --project=. in the clone), since Pkg.add would change the Project.toml of AeroTrixi.jl. In run/, start julia --project=. and execute once\n\nusing Pkg\nPkg.develop(path = \"..\")  # the clone of AeroTrixi.jl (for a released version: Pkg.add(\"AeroTrixi\"))\nPkg.add([\"Trixi\", \"OrdinaryDiffEqLowStorageRK\", \"Gridap\", \"Plots\"])\n\nThis takes a few minutes, mostly for precompilation. run/Project.toml and run/Manifest.toml then record the installed packages; on another machine with a copy of these two files, using Pkg; Pkg.instantiate() installs the same versions.\n\nLater, start julia --project=. in run/ and run the example with\n\nusing Trixi, AeroTrixi\ntrixi_include(joinpath(AeroTrixi.examples_dir(), \"p4est_2d_dgsem\",\n                       \"elixir_euler_poisson_blast_five_trees_amr.jl\"));\n\n(trixi_include returns the solution sol; the semicolon keeps the REPL from printing it) and plot the density with the mesh, the potential, and the field with Plots.jl:\n\nusing Plots\npd = PlotData2D(sol)\np1 = plot(pd[\"rho\"], title = \"density at t = $(sol.t[end])\")\nplot!(p1, getmesh(pd), linecolor = :white, linewidth = 0.3)\np2 = plot(pd[\"phi\"], title = \"potential φ\", color = :balance)\nplot!(p2, getmesh(pd), linecolor = :black, linewidth = 0.2)\np3 = plot(pd[\"E1\"], title = \"E1 = -∂φ/∂x\", color = :balance)\np4 = plot(pd[\"E2\"], title = \"E2 = -∂φ/∂y\", color = :balance)\nplot(p1, p2, p3, p4, layout = (2, 2), size = (1100, 900))\n\n(Image: Density, potential, and field at t = 0.3)\n\nSuggestion: Change coupling = 0 such that the potential follows the density without acting on the flow and you get the pure Euler result for density; at coupling = 1, the density changes by less than 1%.","title":"Example: Euler-Poisson blast wave"},{"category":"section","location":"trixi_gridap_bridge/#Performance","page":"Trixi.jl-Gridap.jl bridge","text":"All timings: Julia 1.13, one thread, after compilation (a short run first).\n\nExample run. 373 time steps up to t = 0.3 (5846 cells at the end), mesh adaptation every 20 steps; 21.6 s in total:\n\npart calls time per call\nTrixi.jl right-hand side 1870 11.0 s 5.9 ms\nGridap.jl stage updates (DG → Q2, solve, Q2 → DG) 1860 5.6 s 3.0 ms\nGridap.jl rebuilds after mesh changes (incl. first) 19 3.2 s 169 ms\nmesh adaptation (Trixi.jl) 18 0.5 s 30 ms\n\nA stage update costs about half of a right-hand side evaluation of Trixi.jl; its main part is the solve with the Cholesky factorization. A rebuild (node numbering, Gridap.jl model and space, assembly and factorization of the matrix, load matrices) costs about as much as one time step.\n\nCosts per call in ms, on the five-tree mesh at the uniform levels 1 to 5 with one more refined ring (hanging nodes):\n\ncells Q2 nodes rebuild stage update: DG → Q2 / load + solve / Q2 → DG Trixi.jl rhs\n773 3197 29 0.51: 0.03 / 0.39 / 0.08 0.75\n3029 12319 94 2.35: 0.20 / 1.82 / 0.34 2.95\n11975 48301 433 8.3: 0.60 / 6.4 / 1.3 10.9\n47777 191909 1830 61: 4.7 / 48 / 8.1 65\n\nThe transfers are a small part of a stage update. In a rebuild, the node numbering and the Gridap.jl model and space take less than 10%; most of the time is spent in the assembly and factorization of the matrix and in the load matrices. The load matrices pay off after 6–11 solves (1–2 time steps with stage updates), compared with assembling the load vector for each solve.\n\nAdaptation interval and refinement. Since each mesh change also rebuilds the finite element data, the interval of the mesh adaptation matters, and so do the thresholds of the refinement. For the example (wall times of the whole run without the analysis and output callbacks; L1 error of the density at t = 0.3 against a coupled uniform run at the finest level 5 with 20480 cells, which takes 92.0 s):\n\nAMR interval AMR thresholds mean / max cells time rebuilds L1 error\n20 0.06 / 0.2 2644 / 3248 12.7 s 19 3.7e-2\n10 0.015 / 0.05 3627 / 5240 20.0 s 37 1.4e-2\n10 0.005 / 0.015 4072 / 5855 22.3 s 38 9.1e-3\n20 0.005 / 0.015 4007 / 5696 19.9 s 19 1.0e-2\n5 0.002 / 0.006 4322 / 6161 32.3 s 75 8.6e-3\n10 0.002 / 0.006 4260 / 6077 23.7 s 38 7.7e-3\n20 0.002 / 0.006 4270 / 6038 20.8 s 19 8.0e-3\n40 0.002 / 0.006 3984 / 6710 16.6 s 10 1.6e-2\nuniform level 4  5120 8.0 s  2.9e-2\n\nThe upper threshold decides the accuracy: the middle threshold (0.002 or 0.005 with the upper threshold 0.015) and the middle level (3 or 4) change the error by less than 10%. Low thresholds refine a wide region around the shock and the waves of the field; then the mesh only needs to be adapted every 20 steps, while after 40 steps the waves leave the refined region and the error doubles. The example uses the thresholds 0.002 / 0.006 and the interval 20: compared with the uniform run at level 4, the error is 3.7 times smaller at 2.6 times the cost (the adaptive mesh needs the time step of level 5), and the run is 4.4 times faster than the uniform run at level 5. Note that the performance of the uniform case is misleading as more complicated geometries and problem setups quickly render uniform refinement infeasible.","title":"Performance"},{"category":"section","location":"trixi_gridap_bridge/#Outlook","page":"Trixi.jl-Gridap.jl bridge","text":"Beyond the limitations listed under \"Scope and limitations\" at the top, natural next steps are:\n\nsystems such as the Stokes problem (vector-valued Q2 fields, a Q1 pressure space, and multi-field spaces), other boundary conditions, and nonlinear elliptic problems;\nlocal time stepping, so that coarse cells take larger time steps, coordinated with the stage updates of the elliptic problem;\nrefinement indicators for the smooth waves of the field, in addition to the shock indicator, so that the waves are not refined to the finest level.","title":"Outlook"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#API-Reference","page":"Overview","text":"The functions and types of AeroTrixi.jl, grouped by topic:\n\nSurface quantities: pointwise aerodynamic coefficients along boundaries\nThermodynamics: tabulated thermodynamic data and models for rotational and vibrational energies\nMulti-species equations: Euler equations for multi-species flows of calorically imperfect gases and their fluxes\nTrixi.jl-Gridap.jl bridge: continuous finite element problems on the adaptive mesh of a DG simulation (see also the guide)","title":"API Reference"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Index","page":"Overview","text":"Pages = [\"api/surface_quantities.md\", \"api/thermodynamics.md\",\n         \"api/multispecies_equations.md\", \"api/trixi_gridap_bridge.md\", \"api.md\"]","title":"Index"},{"category":"module","location":"api/#AeroTrixi.AeroTrixi","page":"Overview","text":"AeroTrixi\n\nHigh-fidelity aerodynamic simulations with Trixi.jl.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AeroTrixi.AeroTrixi"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#AeroTrixi.examples_dir-Tuple{}","page":"Overview","text":"examples_dir()\n\nReturn the directory where the example files provided with AeroTrixi.jl are located. If AeroTrixi.jl is installed as a regular package (with ]add AeroTrixi), these files are read-only and should not be modified. To find out which files are available, use, e.g., readdir:\n\nExamples\n\nreaddir(examples_dir())\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AeroTrixi.examples_dir"},{"category":"section","location":"api/trixi_gridap_bridge/#Trixi.jl-Gridap.jl-bridge","page":"Trixi.jl-Gridap.jl bridge","text":"The functions and types of the bridge between the DG solver of Trixi.jl and continuous finite elements of Gridap.jl. The guide explains the foundations and the usage. The Gridap.jl parts are available when Gridap.jl is loaded (import Gridap).","title":"Trixi.jl-Gridap.jl bridge"},{"category":"section","location":"api/trixi_gridap_bridge/#Meshes","page":"Trixi.jl-Gridap.jl bridge","text":"","title":"Meshes"},{"category":"section","location":"api/trixi_gridap_bridge/#Continuous-Q2-nodes-and-Gridap.jl-spaces","page":"Trixi.jl-Gridap.jl bridge","text":"","title":"Continuous Q2 nodes and Gridap.jl spaces"},{"category":"section","location":"api/trixi_gridap_bridge/#Transfers-between-DG-and-Q2","page":"Trixi.jl-Gridap.jl bridge","text":"","title":"Transfers between DG and Q2"},{"category":"section","location":"api/trixi_gridap_bridge/#Callback-and-elliptic-part-of-equations","page":"Trixi.jl-Gridap.jl bridge","text":"","title":"Callback and elliptic part of equations"},{"category":"section","location":"api/trixi_gridap_bridge/#Euler-Poisson-equations","page":"Trixi.jl-Gridap.jl bridge","text":"","title":"Euler-Poisson equations"},{"category":"method","location":"api/trixi_gridap_bridge/#AeroTrixi.p4est_mesh_five_trees-Tuple{}","page":"Trixi.jl-Gridap.jl bridge","text":"p4est_mesh_five_trees(; l = 0.5, trees_per_block = 1, polydeg, kwargs...)\n\nCreate a 2D P4estMesh of the box [-1, 1]^2 consisting of five blocks: the square [-l, l]^2 in the middle and four trapezoids connecting its sides to the sides of the box. Each block is split into trees_per_block × trees_per_block trees. The connectivity is created with p4est_mesh_from_vertices and contains the full face and corner connectivity.\n\nThe mesh is meant as a simple workhorse for testing features of unstructured meshes:\n\ntrees are non-affine (bilinear) quadrilaterals,\nthe trapezoids are rotated copies of each other, so that the face numbers on both sides of a tree interface differ and some interfaces have flipped orientation,\nthe vertices (±l, ±l) are shared by three trees,\nfor trees_per_block > 1, there are vertices shared by four trees, where diagonally opposite trees only touch at a corner.\n\nThe boundary faces are named :x_neg, :x_pos, :y_neg, and :y_pos. All other keyword arguments are passed to p4est_mesh_from_vertices.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AeroTrixi.p4est_mesh_five_trees"},{"category":"method","location":"api/trixi_gridap_bridge/#AeroTrixi.p4est_mesh_from_vertices-Tuple{AbstractMatrix, AbstractMatrix{<:Integer}}","page":"Trixi.jl-Gridap.jl bridge","text":"p4est_mesh_from_vertices(vertices, tree_to_vertex; polydeg,\n                         boundary_name = Returns(:boundary),\n                         RealT = Float64, initial_refinement_level = 0,\n                         unsaved_changes = true,\n                         p4est_partition_allow_for_coarsening = true)\n\nCreate a 2D P4estMesh from an unstructured list of straight-sided quadrilateral trees.\n\nIn contrast to P4estMesh(trees_per_dimension; ...), the p4est connectivity is derived from the vertex numbering with p4est_connectivity_complete. It therefore contains the full face and corner connectivity between trees, including trees that only touch at a corner and vertices shared by an arbitrary number of trees. A complete connectivity is required by continuous node numberings such as p4est_lnodes.\n\nArguments\n\nvertices::AbstractMatrix: 2 × n_vertices matrix of vertex coordinates.\ntree_to_vertex::AbstractMatrix{<:Integer}: 4 × n_trees matrix of one-based vertex indices. The vertices of each tree are ordered as in p4est (z-order), i.e., (-1, -1), (1, -1), (-1, 1), (1, 1) in the reference coordinates of the tree. Each tree must be right-handed.\npolydeg::Integer: polynomial degree used to store the geometry of the mesh.\nboundary_name: function mapping the midpoint x::SVector{2} of a boundary face to the Symbol used as boundary name. Default: Returns(:boundary).\nRealT::Type: the type that should be used for coordinates. Default: Float64.\ninitial_refinement_level::Integer: refine the mesh uniformly to this level before the simulation starts. Default: 0.\nunsaved_changes::Bool: if set to true, the mesh will be saved to a mesh file. Default: true.\np4est_partition_allow_for_coarsening::Bool: Must be true when using AMR to make mesh adaptivity independent of domain partitioning. Default: true.\n\nPeriodicity is not supported, since it is not inherent in the list of vertices.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AeroTrixi.p4est_mesh_from_vertices"},{"category":"type","location":"api/trixi_gridap_bridge/#AeroTrixi.P4estLnodesQ2","page":"Trixi.jl-Gridap.jl bridge","text":"P4estLnodesQ2(mesh::P4estMesh{2})\n\nContinuous Q2 node numbering of the current forest of a 2D P4estMesh, including hanging nodes, their linear constraints, and boundary labels. It is derived from p4est_lnodes_new(p4est, ghost, 2) and must be rebuilt whenever the mesh changes.\n\nThe nodes of each element are numbered like the nodes of a Trixi.jl element with polydeg = 2, i.e., in lexicographic order of the reference coordinates (-1, 0, 1) with the first coordinate running fastest. All data are stored on the Julia side; the p4est structures are freed after construction. Only serial runs are supported.\n\nFields\n\nelement_nodes::Matrix{Int}: 9 × n_elements node ids of each element, where 1:n_independent are independent nodes and n_independent .+ (1:n_hanging) are hanging nodes.\ncoordinates::Matrix{Float64}: 2 × n_nodes node coordinates.\nn_independent::Int, n_hanging::Int: number of independent and hanging nodes.\nhanging_masters::Matrix{Int}: 3 × n_hanging independent master nodes (a, m, b) of each hanging node, where a and b are the end points of the coarse face and m is its midpoint. Each hanging node lies between a and m, at a quarter of the face.\nhanging_coefficients::Matrix{Float64}: 3 × n_hanging constraint coefficients; the value of hanging node n_independent + k is sum(hanging_coefficients[:, k] .* values[hanging_masters[:, k]]).\nboundary_faces::Vector{Tuple{Int, Int, Symbol}}: (element, face, name) of each element face on the boundary, with faces numbered as in p4est but one-based.\nboundary_entities::Vector{Vector{Symbol}}: distinct sorted sets of boundary names carried by nodes. A node at the junction of two boundaries, e.g., a corner of the domain, belongs to an entity with both names.\nnode_entity::Vector{Int}: index into boundary_entities for each node, 0 for interior nodes.\n\nSee also apply_hanging_constraints!.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AeroTrixi.P4estLnodesQ2"},{"category":"method","location":"api/trixi_gridap_bridge/#AeroTrixi.apply_hanging_constraints!-Tuple{AbstractVector, P4estLnodesQ2}","page":"Trixi.jl-Gridap.jl bridge","text":"apply_hanging_constraints!(values, lnodes::P4estLnodesQ2)\n\nSet the values of the hanging nodes from the values of the independent nodes. values is a vector of length n_independent + n_hanging or a matrix with this number of columns.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AeroTrixi.apply_hanging_constraints!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/trixi_gridap_bridge/#AeroTrixi.gridap_model","page":"Trixi.jl-Gridap.jl bridge","text":"gridap_model(lnodes::P4estLnodesQ2)\n\nCreate a Gridap.jl UnstructuredDiscreteModel of the quadrilaterals of lnodes. The face labeling contains the tags \"interior\", \"boundary\", and one tag for each boundary name of the mesh. Faces and vertices are labeled with the boundary entities of lnodes, so that a vertex at the junction of two boundaries belongs to both tags. Faces at hanging nodes are interior faces.\n\nThis function requires Gridap.jl to be loaded (import Gridap).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AeroTrixi.gridap_model"},{"category":"function","location":"api/trixi_gridap_bridge/#AeroTrixi.gridap_node_values","page":"Trixi.jl-Gridap.jl bridge","text":"gridap_node_values(uh, q2_space)\n\nValues of the Gridap.jl function uh at all nodes of the P4estLnodesQ2 used to create q2_space with gridap_q2_space, including the hanging nodes. The result can be written into a DG solution with transfer_q2_to_dg!.\n\nThis function requires Gridap.jl to be loaded (import Gridap).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AeroTrixi.gridap_node_values"},{"category":"function","location":"api/trixi_gridap_bridge/#AeroTrixi.gridap_q2_function","page":"Trixi.jl-Gridap.jl bridge","text":"gridap_q2_function(node_values, q2_space; field = 1)\n\nCreate a Gridap.jl FEFunction with the values node_values at all nodes of the P4estLnodesQ2 used to create q2_space with gridap_q2_space, e.g., data transferred with transfer_dg_to_q2!. node_values is a vector or a matrix n_fields × n_nodes, of which the row field is used. The function lives in the space without hanging-node constraints, i.e., the values at hanging nodes are used as given; with transfer_dg_to_q2!, they satisfy the constraints.\n\nThis function requires Gridap.jl to be loaded (import Gridap).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AeroTrixi.gridap_q2_function"},{"category":"function","location":"api/trixi_gridap_bridge/#AeroTrixi.gridap_q2_space","page":"Trixi.jl-Gridap.jl bridge","text":"gridap_q2_space(lnodes::P4estLnodesQ2, model; dirichlet_tags = Symbol[])\n\nCreate a continuous Q2 finite element space on the Gridap.jl model created by gridap_model, using the node numbering of lnodes. Nodes carrying one of the boundary names in dirichlet_tags are Dirichlet nodes. Hanging nodes are constrained with FESpaceWithLinearConstraints.\n\nReturn a named tuple with\n\nspace: the constrained space (the unconstrained one if there are no hanging nodes),\nunconstrained: the space with all nodes as degrees of freedom,\nnode_to_dof: degree of freedom of each node in unconstrained, positive for free and negative for Dirichlet degrees of freedom.\n\nThis function requires Gridap.jl to be loaded (import Gridap).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AeroTrixi.gridap_q2_space"},{"category":"type","location":"api/trixi_gridap_bridge/#AeroTrixi.Q2NodeData","page":"Trixi.jl-Gridap.jl bridge","text":"Q2NodeData\n\nQ2 data at a DG node, passed to the function of transfer_q2_to_dg!. Use q2_value and q2_gradient to access the value and the gradient of a field at the node.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AeroTrixi.Q2NodeData"},{"category":"function","location":"api/trixi_gridap_bridge/#AeroTrixi.q2_gradient","page":"Trixi.jl-Gridap.jl bridge","text":"q2_gradient(q::Q2NodeData, field = 1)\n\nPhysical gradient of the Q2 field field at the DG node, i.e., the local derivative within the element, computed with the derivative matrix and metric terms of Trixi.jl. It is discontinuous between elements and only computed when called.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AeroTrixi.q2_gradient"},{"category":"function","location":"api/trixi_gridap_bridge/#AeroTrixi.q2_value","page":"Trixi.jl-Gridap.jl bridge","text":"q2_value(q::Q2NodeData, field = 1)\n\nValue of the Q2 field field at the DG node (injection).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AeroTrixi.q2_value"},{"category":"method","location":"api/trixi_gridap_bridge/#AeroTrixi.transfer_dg_to_q2!-Tuple{Any, AbstractMatrix, Any, Any, P4estLnodesQ2}","page":"Trixi.jl-Gridap.jl bridge","text":"transfer_dg_to_q2!(func, node_values, u_ode, semi, lnodes::P4estLnodesQ2)\n\nTransfer quantities computed from the DG solution u_ode of semi (DG with polydeg = 2) to a continuous Q2 field at the nodes of lnodes.\n\nAt each DG node, func(u_node, x, equations) returns an SVector with one entry per field, e.g., SVector(u_node[1] - 1) for a single field. node_values is a matrix of size n_fields × n_nodes. Each independent node gets the average of the values of all elements sharing it. The hanging nodes then get the values of the quadratic on the coarse face they lie on (see apply_hanging_constraints!), so that the fields are continuous.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AeroTrixi.transfer_dg_to_q2!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/trixi_gridap_bridge/#AeroTrixi.transfer_q2_to_dg!-Tuple{Any, Any, Any, P4estLnodesQ2, AbstractVector}","page":"Trixi.jl-Gridap.jl bridge","text":"transfer_q2_to_dg!(func, u_ode, semi, lnodes::P4estLnodesQ2, node_values)\n\nWrite data computed from Q2 fields at the nodes of lnodes into the DG solution u_ode of semi (DG with polydeg = 2). node_values is a vector (a single field) or a matrix n_fields × n_nodes.\n\nAt each DG node, the new state is func(u_node, q, x, equations), where u_node is the current state and q gives access to the Q2 fields at this node with q2_value and q2_gradient. For example,\n\ntransfer_q2_to_dg!(u_ode, semi, lnodes, φ_nodes) do u, q, x, equations\n    φ = q2_value(q)\n    ∇φ = q2_gradient(q)\n    return prim2cons(SVector(u[1], ∇φ[1], ∇φ[2], φ), equations)\nend\n\nkeeps the density and sets the velocity to ∇φ and the pressure to φ.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AeroTrixi.transfer_q2_to_dg!"},{"category":"type","location":"api/trixi_gridap_bridge/#AeroTrixi.Q2Field","page":"Trixi.jl-Gridap.jl bridge","text":"Q2Field(; components = 1)\n\nField of the elliptic part of equations (see elliptic_fields) in the continuous Q2 space on the nodes of the DG solution. Currently, only scalar fields (components = 1) are supported.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AeroTrixi.Q2Field"},{"category":"function","location":"api/trixi_gridap_bridge/#AeroTrixi.GridapCallback","page":"Trixi.jl-Gridap.jl bridge","text":"GridapCallback(semi; dirichlet_tags = (), interval = 1, quadrature_degree = 4)\n\nCallback that solves the elliptic part of the equations of semi (see elliptic_forms) with continuous Q2 elements in Gridap.jl on the current mesh and writes the result into the DG solution (DG with polydeg = 2). It is called at the start of the simulation and after every interval time steps; place it after an AMRCallback in the CallbackSet, so that the solution is recomputed after mesh changes.\n\nThe sources elliptic_sources are evaluated at the DG nodes and transferred to the Q2 nodes with transfer_dg_to_q2!.\nThe weak forms of elliptic_forms are integrated with quadrature_degree, with homogeneous Dirichlet conditions on the boundaries named in dirichlet_tags. The linear form must be affine in the sources.\nThe solution is written into the DG solution with write_elliptic_solution and transfer_q2_to_dg!.\n\nAfter each mesh change (detected with the revision counter of p4est), the callback rebuilds the node numbering, the Gridap.jl model and space, the matrix with its factorization (Cholesky if elliptic_spd, LU otherwise), and the matrices that map the source values to the load vector. A solve then only needs sparse matrix-vector products and the solve with the factorization.\n\nWith the callback alone, the solution is updated once per time step and constant during the Runge-Kutta stages, which limits the coupled time integration to first order. For the full order of the method, update it on each stage with gridap_stage_update; the callback then only acts at the start and after mesh changes.\n\nThis function requires Gridap.jl to be loaded (import Gridap).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AeroTrixi.GridapCallback"},{"category":"function","location":"api/trixi_gridap_bridge/#AeroTrixi.elliptic_fields","page":"Trixi.jl-Gridap.jl bridge","text":"elliptic_fields(equations)\n\nUnknowns of the elliptic part of equations as a named tuple of their spaces, e.g., (phi = Q2Field(),) for a potential. A system such as the Stokes problem would have several fields, e.g., a velocity and a pressure.\n\nEquations with an elliptic part implement elliptic_fields, elliptic_sources, elliptic_forms, and write_elliptic_solution, and optionally elliptic_spd; a GridapCallback then solves the elliptic part with GridapCallback(semi; dirichlet_tags).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AeroTrixi.elliptic_fields"},{"category":"function","location":"api/trixi_gridap_bridge/#AeroTrixi.elliptic_forms","page":"Trixi.jl-Gridap.jl bridge","text":"elliptic_forms(equations, gridap)\n\nWeak forms (a, l) of the elliptic part of equations, written with the operators of the Gridap.jl module gridap (e.g., ∫, ∇, ⋅ = gridap.∫, gridap.∇, gridap.:⋅), so that the equations do not depend on Gridap.jl:\n\na(us, vs, dΩ): bilinear form for the tuples of unknowns us and test functions vs (one entry per field of elliptic_fields),\nl(vs, sources, dΩ): linear form with the source fields sources[k] (see elliptic_sources); it must be affine in the sources.\n\ndΩ is the measure of the mesh. Dirichlet conditions are homogeneous, on the boundaries given to the GridapCallback.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AeroTrixi.elliptic_forms"},{"category":"function","location":"api/trixi_gridap_bridge/#AeroTrixi.elliptic_sources","page":"Trixi.jl-Gridap.jl bridge","text":"elliptic_sources(u, x, equations)\n\nSource fields of the elliptic part of equations at a DG node with the state u and the coordinates x, as an SVector; they are transferred to the Q2 nodes and passed to the linear form of elliptic_forms.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AeroTrixi.elliptic_sources"},{"category":"method","location":"api/trixi_gridap_bridge/#AeroTrixi.elliptic_spd-Tuple{Any}","page":"Trixi.jl-Gridap.jl bridge","text":"elliptic_spd(equations)\n\nWhether the matrix of the elliptic part of equations is symmetric positive definite, so that it is factorized with Cholesky instead of LU. Default: false.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AeroTrixi.elliptic_spd"},{"category":"function","location":"api/trixi_gridap_bridge/#AeroTrixi.gridap_stage_update","page":"Trixi.jl-Gridap.jl bridge","text":"gridap_stage_update(gridap_callback)\n\nReturn the update of a GridapCallback for use as stage_limiter! of the time integration method, e.g.,\n\nCarpenterKennedy2N54(; stage_limiter! = gridap_stage_update(gridap_callback),\n                     williamson_condition = false)\n\n(SSPRK and low-storage methods of OrdinaryDiffEq.jl; with williamson_condition = true, the stage limiter is not called). The elliptic problem is then solved on each stage value before the right-hand side is evaluated, and on the solution at the end of each step, with the cached matrix factorization. The callback is switched to act only at the start and after mesh changes, where it rebuilds the cached data and updates the solution on the new mesh; keep it after the AMRCallback in the CallbackSet.\n\nThis function requires Gridap.jl to be loaded (import Gridap).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AeroTrixi.gridap_stage_update"},{"category":"function","location":"api/trixi_gridap_bridge/#AeroTrixi.write_elliptic_solution","page":"Trixi.jl-Gridap.jl bridge","text":"write_elliptic_solution(u, q, x, equations)\n\nNew state at a DG node with the state u and the coordinates x from the solution of the elliptic part of equations; q2_value(q, k) and q2_gradient(q, k) give the field k of elliptic_fields and its gradient at the node.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AeroTrixi.write_elliptic_solution"},{"category":"type","location":"api/trixi_gridap_bridge/#AeroTrixi.CompressibleEulerPoissonEquations2D","page":"Trixi.jl-Gridap.jl bridge","text":"CompressibleEulerPoissonEquations2D(gamma; coupling = 0, rho_0 = 1)\n\nThe compressible Euler equations in 2D of a charged gas: a single charged species in a uniform neutralizing background of density rho_0, under its own electrostatic potential phi and field E = -nabla phi,\n\nbeginaligned\npartial_t rho + nabla cdot (rho v) = 0 \npartial_t (rho v) + nabla cdot (rho v otimes v + p I) = c  rho E \npartial_t (rho e) + nabla cdot ((rho e + p) v) = c  rho v cdot E \n-Delta phi = rho - rho_0 qquad E = -nabla phi\nendaligned\n\nwith the coupling constant c (coupling) and the background density rho_0 (rho_0). The field accelerates the gas and does work on it; for c  0, it pushes the gas from regions of excess density towards regions of deficit. With the charge-to-mass ratio qm of the species and the permittivity varepsilon_0, the physical potential is phi_mathrmphys = (qm)  varepsilon_0  phi, and\n\nc = frac(qm)^2varepsilon_0 = fracomega_p^2rho_0\n\nwith the plasma frequency omega_p of the background. A negative coupling c = -4 pi G describes self-gravity instead (attraction instead of repulsion).\n\nThe variables are (rho rho v_1 rho v_2 rho e phi E_1 E_2). The Euler part is the one of CompressibleEulerEquations2D(gamma). The potential and the field have no flux and no numerical dissipation, so that they are not changed by the time integration of the DG discretization. They are set by the solution of the elliptic part of the equations, the Poisson problem, which a GridapCallback solves with continuous finite elements (see elliptic_forms). The field acts on the flow through the source terms source_terms_euler_poisson.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AeroTrixi.CompressibleEulerPoissonEquations2D"},{"category":"method","location":"api/trixi_gridap_bridge/#AeroTrixi.source_terms_euler_poisson-Tuple{Any, Any, Any, CompressibleEulerPoissonEquations2D}","page":"Trixi.jl-Gridap.jl bridge","text":"source_terms_euler_poisson(u, x, t, equations::CompressibleEulerPoissonEquations2D)\n\nForce of the electric field on the flow: c rho E in the momentum equations and its work c rho v cdot E in the energy equation, with the coupling constant c = equations.coupling. For a charged gas with the charge-to-mass ratio qm and the permittivity varepsilon_0, c = (qm)^2  varepsilon_0 = omega_p^2  rho_0 with the plasma frequency omega_p of the background density rho_0 (see CompressibleEulerPoissonEquations2D). The potential and the field have no source terms; they are set by the solution of the elliptic part of the equations.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AeroTrixi.source_terms_euler_poisson"},{"category":"section","location":"api/thermodynamics/#Thermodynamics","page":"Thermodynamics","text":"ThermoData1T holds tabulated internal energies and specific heats of multi-species flows in thermal equilibrium, with interpolation and the inversion for the temperature. The rotational and vibrational contributions come from the models below.","title":"Thermodynamics"},{"category":"section","location":"api/thermodynamics/#Tabulated-thermodynamic-data","page":"Thermodynamics","text":"","title":"Tabulated thermodynamic data"},{"category":"section","location":"api/thermodynamics/#Rotational-and-vibrational-energies","page":"Thermodynamics","text":"","title":"Rotational and vibrational energies"},{"category":"type","location":"api/thermodynamics/#AeroTrixi.ThermoData1T","page":"Thermodynamics","text":"ThermoData1T(ref_q, mass_arr, e_c_int_function_arr;\n             T_min = 10.0, T_max = 3.0e4, T_tol = 1e-9, dT = 1.0,\n             interpolation = :linear, cv_table_offset = false)\n\nTabulated thermodynamic data for a flow of NCOMP species in thermal equilibrium, i.e. a single temperature T shared by all degrees of freedom.\n\nFor each species i the specific internal energy and the specific heat at constant volume are split into a translational part, which is added internally, and a part carrying the internal degrees of freedom, which is supplied by the caller:\n\ne_i(T) = frac32 frack_B Tm_i + e_i^textint(T)\nqquad\nc_vi(T) = frac32 frack_Bm_i + c_vi^textint(T)\n\nBoth are sampled on a uniform grid of step Delta T and evaluated by linear interpolation. The entropy integral\n\nint_T_0^T fracc_vi(tau)tau  mathrmdtau\n\nis accumulated over the tabulation points and completed with an exact partial-cell contribution, T_0 being the first point of the c_v grid.\n\nThe energy table holds N_T points at T_min + (i-1)Delta T. Where the c_v table lives is set by cv_table_offset:\n\nfalse (NoCvOffset): the same grid as the energy table.\ntrue (CvOffset): a grid shifted by -Delta T2, i.e.   T_min - Delta T2 + (i-1)Delta T with N_T + 2 points. The two extra   points keep T_min T_max strictly bracketed. Sampling c_v at cell   midpoints makes it consistent with the slope of the piecewise-linear energy.\n\nBoth index pairs are returned together by get_index_lower_fracpos; with an offset table they differ, and mixing them up is silently wrong.\n\nT(e) is inverted by a Newton iteration with tolerance T_tol, clamped to 10001T_min 09999T_max.\n\nAll stored quantities are dimensionless, scaled by the ReferenceFlowQuantities instance ref_q, which is retained in the ref_q field.\n\nArguments\n\nref_q: ReferenceFlowQuantities used to non-dimensionalise every table.\nmass_arr: species masses in kg. Not modified.\ne_c_int_function_arr: one callable per species, (m, T) -> (e, c_v), returning   the internal-degree-of-freedom contributions only. The translational parts must   not be included.\nT_min, T_max, dT: tabulation range and step, in K.\nT_tol: relative tolerance of the Newton solver for T(e).\ninterpolation: only :linear is implemented.\ncv_table_offset: place c_v on the midpoint grid, see above.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AeroTrixi.ThermoData1T"},{"category":"type","location":"api/thermodynamics/#AeroTrixi.CvOffset","page":"Thermodynamics","text":"CvOffset <: CvTableOffset\n\nTabulate c_v(T) at cell midpoints, T_min - Delta T2 + i Delta T, i.e. offset by half a step from the internal energy grid. See ThermoData1T.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AeroTrixi.CvOffset"},{"category":"type","location":"api/thermodynamics/#AeroTrixi.LinearInterpolation","page":"Thermodynamics","text":"LinearInterpolation <: Interpolation\n\nLinear interpolation in the temperature tables of ThermoData1T.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AeroTrixi.LinearInterpolation"},{"category":"type","location":"api/thermodynamics/#AeroTrixi.NoCvOffset","page":"Thermodynamics","text":"NoCvOffset <: CvTableOffset\n\nTabulate c_v(T) at the same temperatures as the internal energy, T_min + i Delta T. See ThermoData1T.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AeroTrixi.NoCvOffset"},{"category":"method","location":"api/thermodynamics/#AeroTrixi.c_rot_cont-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"Thermodynamics","text":"c_rot_cont(m, T)\n\nRotational specific heat (J/(kg K)) for a continuous, fully excited spectrum; see e_rot_cont.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AeroTrixi.c_rot_cont"},{"category":"method","location":"api/thermodynamics/#AeroTrixi.c_vibr_from_array-Tuple{Any, Any, Any}","page":"Thermodynamics","text":"c_vibr_from_array(m, E_vibr_array_K, Tv)\n\nVibrational specific heat (J/(kg K)) from the level energies E_vibr_array_K (K) with a Boltzmann distribution; see e_vibr_from_array.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AeroTrixi.c_vibr_from_array"},{"category":"method","location":"api/thermodynamics/#AeroTrixi.c_vibr_iho-Tuple{Any, Any, Any}","page":"Thermodynamics","text":"c_vibr_iho(m, Θ, Tv)\n\nVibrational specific heat (J/(kg K)) of the infinite harmonic oscillator; see e_vibr_iho.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AeroTrixi.c_vibr_iho"},{"category":"method","location":"api/thermodynamics/#AeroTrixi.e_rot_cont-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"Thermodynamics","text":"e_rot_cont(m, T)\n\nSpecific rotational energy (J/kg) of a particle of mass m (kg) at temperature T (K), for a continuous, fully excited spectrum.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AeroTrixi.e_rot_cont"},{"category":"method","location":"api/thermodynamics/#AeroTrixi.e_vibr_from_array-Tuple{Any, Any, Any}","page":"Thermodynamics","text":"e_vibr_from_array(m, E_vibr_array_K, Tv)\n\nSpecific vibrational energy (J/kg) from the level energies E_vibr_array_K (K), averaged with a Boltzmann distribution at the vibrational temperature Tv (K).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AeroTrixi.e_vibr_from_array"},{"category":"method","location":"api/thermodynamics/#AeroTrixi.e_vibr_iho-Tuple{Any, Any, Any}","page":"Thermodynamics","text":"e_vibr_iho(m, Θ, Tv)\n\nSpecific vibrational energy (J/kg) of the infinite harmonic oscillator with the characteristic temperature Θ (K) at the vibrational temperature Tv (K).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AeroTrixi.e_vibr_iho"},{"category":"method","location":"api/thermodynamics/#AeroTrixi.generate_e_c_rot_cont-Tuple{}","page":"Thermodynamics","text":"generate_e_c_rot_cont()\n\nFunction (m, T) -> (e, c) with the rotational energy and specific heat of e_rot_cont and c_rot_cont.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AeroTrixi.generate_e_c_rot_cont"},{"category":"method","location":"api/thermodynamics/#AeroTrixi.generate_e_c_vibr_from_array-Tuple{Any}","page":"Thermodynamics","text":"generate_e_c_vibr_from_array(E_vibr_array_K)\n\nFunction (m, Tv) -> (e, c) with the vibrational energy and specific heat of e_vibr_from_array and c_vibr_from_array for the given level energies.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AeroTrixi.generate_e_c_vibr_from_array"},{"category":"method","location":"api/thermodynamics/#AeroTrixi.generate_e_c_vibr_iho-Tuple{Any}","page":"Thermodynamics","text":"generate_e_c_vibr_iho(Θ)\n\nFunction (m, Tv) -> (e, c) with the vibrational energy and specific heat of e_vibr_iho and c_vibr_iho for the characteristic temperature Θ.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AeroTrixi.generate_e_c_vibr_iho"},{"category":"method","location":"api/thermodynamics/#AeroTrixi.generate_e_vibr_arr_anharmonic_cutoff_K-Tuple{Any, Any, Any}","page":"Thermodynamics","text":"generate_e_vibr_arr_anharmonic_cutoff_K(Θ, Θ_anh, E_diss; ground_level_energy_zero = true)\n\nAnharmonic level energies (K), E_i = (i + 12) Θ - (i + 12)^2 Θ_anh, below E_diss (K) and up to the maximum of the quadratic spectrum, beyond which the energies would decrease with i and the approximation loses validity; see generate_e_vibr_arr_harmonic_cutoff_K.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AeroTrixi.generate_e_vibr_arr_anharmonic_cutoff_K"},{"category":"method","location":"api/thermodynamics/#AeroTrixi.generate_e_vibr_arr_harmonic_cutoff_K-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"Thermodynamics","text":"generate_e_vibr_arr_harmonic_cutoff_K(Θ, E_diss; ground_level_energy_zero = true)\n\nVibrational level energies (K) of the harmonic oscillator, E_i = (i + 12) Θ, below the dissociation energy E_diss (K); shifted to E_0 = 0 if ground_level_energy_zero.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AeroTrixi.generate_e_vibr_arr_harmonic_cutoff_K"},{"category":"section","location":"#AeroTrixi.jl","page":"Home","text":"AeroTrixi.jl is a package for high-fidelity aerodynamic simulations built on top of Trixi.jl. It adds pointwise surface quantities for aerodynamic applications, thermodynamics and entropy-conservative fluxes for multi-species flows of calorically imperfect gases, and a bridge to continuous finite elements of Gridap.jl for hyperbolic systems coupled to elliptic equations. In the future, we plan to add more features for nonideal and rarefied gases, for instance.","title":"AeroTrixi.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"#Features","page":"Home","text":"Surface pressure and friction coefficients: the SurfacePointwiseCallback computes and saves pointwise aerodynamic coefficients along boundaries, such as SurfacePressureCoefficient and SurfaceFrictionCoefficient (see Surface quantities).\nThermodynamic data: tabulated internal energies and specific heats with interpolation and temperature inversion routines for multi-species flows in thermal equilibrium via ThermoData1T, with models for rotational and vibrational energies (see Thermodynamics).\nEntropy-conservative/stable fluxes for non-calorically perfect multi-species flows: CompressibleEulerEquationsMs1T2D for 2D multi-species flows based on tabulated thermodynamic data, with the entropy-conservative flux flux_oblapenko_etal and its faster approximation flux_oblapenko_etal_taylor; chemical reactions are possible via source terms (see Multi-species equations).\nTrixi.jl-Gridap.jl bridge: continuous finite element problems, assembled with Gridap.jl, solved on the adaptive P4estMesh of a DG simulation (2D, DG with polydeg = 2 and Q2 elements), with sources from and results written into the DG solution, on each Runge-Kutta stage via the GridapCallback; for example, the Euler equations of a charged gas, CompressibleEulerPoissonEquations2D (see the guide and the API).","title":"Features"},{"category":"section","location":"#Installation","page":"Home","text":"AeroTrixi.jl is registered in the Julia General Registry. Install it using:\n\nusing Pkg\nPkg.add(\"AeroTrixi\")","title":"Installation"},{"category":"section","location":"#Examples","page":"Home","text":"To get started, it is best to take a look at the examples in the folder returned by examples_dir, which run with trixi_include like the examples of Trixi.jl:\n\nFeature Examples\nAerodynamic flows and surface quantities p4est_2d_dgsem/elixir_euler_NACA0012airfoil_mach08.jl, p4est_2d_dgsem/elixir_navierstokes_NACA0012airfoil_mach08.jl, p4est_3d_dgsem/elixir_euler_ONERA_M6_AVI.jl (with surface coefficients), p4est_3d_dgsem/elixir_euler_DPW3_wing.jl\nMulti-species flows tree_2d_dgsem/elixir_eulermst1T_ec.jl (entropy conservation), tree_2d_dgsem/elixir_eulermst1T_chem.jl (dissociation), p4est_2d_dgsem/elixir_eulerms1T_1speciescylinder.jl, p4est_2d_dgsem/elixir_eulerms1T_2speciescylinder_chem.jl (hypersonic cylinder)\nTrixi.jl-Gridap.jl bridge p4est_2d_dgsem/elixir_euler_poisson_blast_five_trees_amr.jl","title":"Examples"},{"category":"section","location":"#Documentation","page":"Home","text":"The guide to the Trixi.jl-Gridap.jl bridge explains its foundations and usage. The API Reference documents all functions and types, grouped by topic.","title":"Documentation"},{"category":"section","location":"#Credit","page":"Home","text":"","title":"Credit"},{"category":"section","location":"#Referencing","page":"Home","text":"If you use AeroTrixi.jl in your research, you should cite the upstream Trixi.jl repository:\n\n@article{ranocha2022adaptive,\n  title={Adaptive numerical simulations with {T}rixi.jl:\n         {A} case study of {J}ulia for scientific computing},\n  author={Ranocha, Hendrik and Schlottke-Lakemper, Michael and Winters, Andrew Ross\n          and Faulhaber, Erik and Chan, Jesse and Gassner, Gregor},\n  journal={Proceedings of the JuliaCon Conferences},\n  volume={1},\n  number={1},\n  pages={77},\n  year={2022},\n  doi={10.21105/jcon.00077},\n  eprint={2108.06476},\n  eprinttype={arXiv},\n  eprintclass={cs.MS}\n}\n\n@article{schlottkelakemper2021purely,\n  title={A purely hyperbolic discontinuous {G}alerkin approach for\n         self-gravitating gas dynamics},\n  author={Schlottke-Lakemper, Michael and Winters, Andrew R and\n          Ranocha, Hendrik and Gassner, Gregor J},\n  journal={Journal of Computational Physics},\n  pages={110467},\n  year={2021},\n  month={06},\n  volume={442},\n  publisher={Elsevier},\n  doi={10.1016/j.jcp.2021.110467},\n  eprint={2008.10593},\n  eprinttype={arXiv},\n  eprintclass={math.NA}\n}\n\nIn addition, you can also refer to Trixi.jl directly as\n\n@misc{schlottkelakemper2025trixi,\n  title={{T}rixi.jl: {A}daptive high-order numerical simulations\n         of hyperbolic {PDE}s in {J}ulia},\n  author={Schlottke-Lakemper, Michael and Gassner, Gregor J and\n          Ranocha, Hendrik and Winters, Andrew R and Chan, Jesse\n          and Rueda-Ramírez, Andrés},\n  year={2025},\n  howpublished={\\url{https://github.com/trixi-framework/Trixi.jl}},\n  doi={10.5281/zenodo.3996439}\n}\n\nIf using the entropy-conservative multi-species fluxes provided with CompressibleEulerEquationsMs1T2D, please also cite\n\n@article{oblapenko2025entropyconservative,\n  title={Entropy-conservative high-order methods for high-enthalpy gas flows},\n  author={Oblapenko, Georgii and Torrilhon, Manuel},\n  journal={Computers \\& Fluids},\n  volume={295},\n  pages={106640},\n  year={2025},\n  publisher={Elsevier},\n  doi={10.1016/j.compfluid.2025.106640}\n}\n\n@inproceedings{oblapenko2024entropy,\n  title={Entropy-stable fluxes for high-order Discontinuous Galerkin simulations of high-enthalpy flows},\n  author={Oblapenko, Georgii and Tarnovskiy, Arseniy and Ertl, Moritz and Torrilhon, Manuel},\n  booktitle={STAB/DGLR Symposium 2024},\n  pages={393--402},\n  year={2026},\n  organization={Springer},\n  doi={10.1007/978-3-032-11115-9_36}\n}","title":"Referencing"},{"category":"section","location":"#Acknowledgements","page":"Home","text":"Furthermore, a lot of repository logistics and their structure (GitHub CI, testing, docs, etc.) are taken straight from Trixi.jl. Thus, we greatly acknowledge all efforts from the Trixi.jl Authors.\n\nWe also thank Arpit Babbar who provided the first implementation of point-wise analysis quantities.","title":"Acknowledgements"},{"category":"section","location":"api/surface_quantities/#Surface-quantities","page":"Surface quantities","text":"AeroTrixi.jl computes pointwise aerodynamic coefficients, such as the pressure and the skin friction coefficient, along boundaries and saves them with the SurfacePointwiseCallback.","title":"Surface quantities"},{"category":"type","location":"api/surface_quantities/#AeroTrixi.SurfacePointwiseCallback","page":"Surface quantities","text":"SurfacePointwiseCallback(semi, quantities::SurfacePointwise...; interval)\n\nCompute and save pointwise surface quantities, such as the SurfacePressureCoefficient or the SurfaceFrictionCoefficient, at the start of the simulation, every interval time steps, and at the end. Each quantity is given as an SurfacePointwise, which determines the boundaries and the output directory; the values at all surface nodes are written to <output directory>/<variable name>_<time step>.h5, e.g., out/CP_x_000100.h5.\n\nThe quantities are computed from an evaluation of the right-hand side at the current time, which provides the solution at the boundaries and, for viscous quantities such as the friction coefficient, the gradients of the solution.\n\nThis callback replaces the keyword argument analysis_pointwise of the AnalysisCallback of AeroTrixi.jl v0.4 and is used together with the AnalysisCallback of Trixi.jl.\n\nExample\n\npressure_coefficient = SurfacePointwise((:airfoil,),\n                                                SurfacePressureCoefficient(p_inf, rho_inf,\n                                                                           u_inf))\nsurface_pointwise_callback = SurfacePointwiseCallback(semi, pressure_coefficient;\n                                                      interval = 100)\ncallbacks = CallbackSet(summary_callback, analysis_callback, surface_pointwise_callback)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AeroTrixi.SurfacePointwiseCallback"},{"category":"method","location":"api/surface_quantities/#AeroTrixi.SurfaceFrictionCoefficient-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"Surface quantities","text":"SurfaceFrictionCoefficient(rhoinf, uinf)\n\nCompute the surface skin friction coefficient\n\nC_f coloneqq fracboldsymbol tau_w  boldsymbol n^perp\n                     05 rho_infty U_infty^2 L_infty\n\nbased on the wall shear stress vector tau_w along a boundary. Supposed to be used in conjunction with SurfacePointwise which stores the boundary information and semidiscretization.\n\nrho_inf::Real: Free-stream density\nu_inf::Real: Free-stream velocity\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AeroTrixi.SurfaceFrictionCoefficient"},{"category":"type","location":"api/surface_quantities/#AeroTrixi.SurfacePointwise","page":"Surface quantities","text":"SurfacePointwise{Variable, NBoundaries}(boundary_symbol_or_boundary_symbols,\n                                                variable, output_directory = \"out\")\n\nThis struct is used to compute pointwise surface values of a quantity of interest variable alongside the boundary/boundaries associated with particular names given in boundary_symbols. For instance, this can be used to compute the surface pressure coefficient SurfacePressureCoefficient or surface friction coefficient SurfaceFrictionCoefficient of e.g. an 2D airfoil with the boundary names :AirfoilTop, :AirfoilBottom which would be supplied as  boundary_symbols = (:AirfoilTop, :AirfoilBottom). A single boundary name can also be supplied, e.g. boundary_symbols = (:AirfoilTop,).\n\nboundary_symbols::NTuple{NBoundaries, Symbol}: Name(s) of the boundary/boundaries where the quantity of interest is computed\nvariable::Variable: Quantity of interest, like lift or drag\noutput_directory = \"out\": Directory where the pointwise value files are stored.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AeroTrixi.SurfacePointwise"},{"category":"method","location":"api/surface_quantities/#AeroTrixi.SurfacePressureCoefficient-Tuple{Any, Any, Any}","page":"Surface quantities","text":"SurfacePressureCoefficient(p_inf, rho_inf, u_inf)\n\nCompute the surface pressure coefficient\n\nC_p coloneqq fracp - p_infty\n                     05 rho_infty U_infty^2\n\nbased on the pressure distribution along a boundary. Supposed to be used in conjunction with SurfacePointwise which stores the boundary information and semidiscretization.\n\np_inf::Real: Free-stream pressure\nrho_inf::Real: Free-stream density\nu_inf::Real: Free-stream velocity\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AeroTrixi.SurfacePressureCoefficient"}]
}
